\pard\plain \fi720\sl288\slmult1\ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Love can only land if it is looked for. This was one life lesson which stayed steady across all her years. A love sent must land, or else it wheels about, and like a shorebird blown to sea it will panic, tire, then drown. The only way to avoid that grief is to never love, but that is difficult to do, so the next best thing is to never love unless it is looked for.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Wait until the beloved }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1\cf1 wants}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1  it,\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8221\'94 is what she preached, even if she could not practice it. She was the Impartial Third, and being impartial, quintessentially so, she loved everyone and everything intensely. That nothing loved her back, she could carry. She could. It was only grief. But it was a grief upon grief, for there was another life lesson which stayed steady across all her years, though it could not }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1\cf1 quite}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1  be called a lesson, for she never did learn how to stop those whom she loved from making mistakes.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 She could only watch.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 An uncountable number of cycles, watching. That took its toll.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Now she watched herself, disassociated. She watched her legs walk her own body up to the edge of a cliff. She looked down and saw the shape that her skull would make after bursting on a sandstone boulder far below. The rest of her skinny self would break with a sickening crack in that shallow ravine. The red of the iron rich clay would hide the spill of blood, but her white robes would wear it well. Tomorrow\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s sun would find her there. She would be that old woman flyswatted at the base of a cliff. She was a seeress, and possessed the sight, and so she saw this fate as readily as if it had already happened. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 She saw how, on some future day, a chance rain would wash over the black peaks behind and sweep her bones out into the broader expanse of the desert. There, she would join two armies who had, in the dark of night, slaughtered each other. She had loved them, and they\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d slaughtered each other. Thousands lay scattered on the white sand, dark lumps in the red morning, wastes of mother\u8217\'92s milk. Why? Pick the reason. Some Lord desired grazing lands which belonged to his neighbors. Or perhaps grazing lands were overgrazed, and now all must contest for scraps. A simple insult might have forced this day, or jealousy, or a pattern of abuse inflicted on a child who became king, growing him into a black hole of need. What did the cause matter when the consequences were always the same? Rape. Base enslavement. Slaughter. And who could intervene when the prayers of the oppressor were addressed to the same god as the oppressed? She could do nothing about it but plant cautionary tales and watch as the centuries twisted her words into the exact opposite of what she\u8217\'92d meant.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Look at them.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Those square battle standards were planted in the mounds of caliche }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1\cf1 this morning}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 , and already, the sun had bleached the color. One full day of bright light working on a weak dye, and those honored sons and daughters could no longer say who had been fighting who, not with gusts of sand piling into their mouths.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 She loved them as her}{\f1\fs24\b0\i1\cf1  own children}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 , and sand piled into their mouths.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93It must finish!\u8221\'94 she cried out to the east, to the rising sun, to the biting wind. \u8220\'93Or else, return to the way it was!\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The wind knew her meaning. It carried the trace scent of wildflowers on its breath. It remembered how the riverbed below her mountain was once flush with water, how that broad expanse of desert was once green and verged with fields of thumb-sized flower bells, swinging, blue and pink. It teased her with that aroma, it stuck that sweetness to her skin, and oh, how she would delight in the excitement of every little butterfly and bee, if only those crazed swarms weren\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t ten thousand years gone. Gone like the red-headed birds who nibbled on them. Gone like the foxes who hunted both bird and bee. Gone were the happy tribes who praised only the sun.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Who was responsible for all this disappearance?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 She blamed herself entirely. She was the Third of the three fateweavers. The Creative First and Destructive Second played their role; they measured out competing lengths of fate for each creature\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s life, but as the Impartial Third, it was her hand that guided the cutting knife. Being Impartial, she cut each thread of fate halfway down the difference. It had been her duty, and now it was her shame, because the only life native to this part of the world now were lichens. Brown, fuzzy lichens were the only things that lived because they were the only things that could find water by sipping on the morning fog.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Last as they were, she loved those lichens. They\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d been her consolation; a strange form of life, beautiful in its own way and totally unique to this small patch of the world. They were enough to smile at. But interspersed as they were amongst the embattled dead, they\u8217\'92d soaked up too much blood, or else were trampled. Too many of their number would die. This day was the first link in a chain of catastrophes which would see their species extinct in less than twenty years.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 She stepped off the cliff.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Her white robes clung to her body as she fell. The wind ripped tears from her eyes while the whistling in her ears promised death.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 As ever, she thought of those she loved. And as ever, for them, she needed to see the day through.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 She clenched her fists. Tear-blind, she swerved out of her fall with the power of flight. She zipped through the red rock valley. The fossilizing river opened up into a dessicated lake, where the sweep of embattled dead grew into a multitude, their faces buried in competing dreams of sacrifice.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 If only these honored sons and daughters could see the red line of fire which scored the horizon, marking another outbreak of war which would burn for days. A roiling cloud of smoke spread out above that conflict, drawn from the ashes of a city. That cloud was an immensity so great that it could be called a god in its own right, deserving of a name. L}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 ightning flashed inside its stomach. On came the distant drum of sequent thunder, and oh, how it mocked those sacrificial dreams\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 they died for what?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 She bit her lip to keep from weeping.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Doubling her purpose}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 , she took a deep breath, whispered a charm to trap a wide bubble of air around her, then punched straight up into the sky.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 In half an instant, she was swimming in the peachy glow of the middle reaches. She paused here and sat on a cloud to spy the sunny side of the world. On that eastern rim, the land curved away in shades of green. A shining river delta ran through it. The Creative First built out that alluvial plain with a word she\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d cobbled together from invented syllables, and she worked there now, hoping to start history anew. In a few years, a new }{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 first king and a new first queen would pull themselves out of the muck, along with a new priestly class, with new temptations, and new spiritual debts. A hundred years would see a new first demagogue. In two hundred, the first silver coin and the first imitation of it, which would be a thin bit of copper washed in pewter, and that coin would end up inside the same hoard as the first bejeweled crown. One did not need the sight to foretell new wars, new famines, new hates, loves, children, feasts, all of it. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Far to the west, the desert curved away into night. A shifting layer of blurring cloud and smoke obscured a smattering of tiny fires. The Destructive Second lurked there, cracking open mountains to draw up heat. A volcano erupted while her minions spread her message of love and hate and sacrifice to every tribe that could no longer stand the endless sink of history. In one hundred years that land would would be black with ash. In two hundred, it would be green with new, ignorant growth, and the whole cycle would start anew.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The Impartial Third drifted below the cloud bottom. From this height, those embattled dead, those honored sons and daughters, they looked like stone debris spilling off the broken walls of their gray city. To their north, a swarm of horsemen drove a herd of cattle into forest lands freshly slashed and burned. }{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 The principal river running through that land had been dammed to trap a lake whose waterline lay far below the yellow stain of its highest mark. Abutting the lake\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s eastern rim was a line of bare mountains, called the Gokree mountains. The Gokree stretched out into the desert, then south, all the way down to the sea. These red mountains rumpled up the rifts of two tectonic plates like festering wounds, and behold, in the middle of all that scabby earthflesh, standing on the highest peak, was the God of all creation. He, a fallen star, four pointed, was a pure emittance of white light which softly split into rays of red, green, and blue}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 . From this height, she could blot out His radiance with her thumb.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 This love hurt the most. He should be that being which made safe all love put out into the world. There shouldn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t be such a thing as unrequited love, not when He walked the world. He should be that backstop. Instead He simply stood there and watched grief pile upon grief.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Because of Him, it must finish.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 She flew straight up through the warm gradations of the sunrise to find the black of night, where the chill gripped her. The object she sought lay still higher. She had placed it there in the upper reaches.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 She slowed to a drift. Her gray braids slackened in this weaker gravity, then brushed past her neck. Her robes lifted off her skin like a waking flower. And now she could sense the gnawing ache, the mood which told her she was getting close. If she wasn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t careful she might collide with it.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 She stopped. The skirt of her robes flowed past her like a white sail, then brushed up against some invisible protrusion in the stars. The cloth folded around it, this thing, this invisible object sitting in the void. It only became visible if she focused on her grief.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 She thought of those honored sons and daughters. She dwelt in that vision of her own dead body at the bottom of the cliff. Tears welled in her eyes, and through this blurring lens she saw an edge shimmer. At once, the fullness of the thing she sought shifted into being.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 What it was, she had no name for, so she called it Black. It was a jagged black stone. It\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s shattered surface distorted the milky swathe of stars around it, while its body was chaotically pinched, the shape of a dagger wound, a poorly healed scar contracture marring the skin of night. Its size was impossible to say, as the Black was of a substance which defied all size comparison. It would appear larger next to objectively larger things, for it could reach straight into her mind to impose a feeling of being insignificant, and purposeless. Its essence overwhelmed hers.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The Black turned, slowly, through its tempted orbit. It turned to reveal a flat, cylindrical head, a shape suggesting it should slot into something greater, and perhaps it could. For her purposes, that head would serve as a hammer.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 She floated around the Black\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s massive body to take up her position behind it. By now, her tears were frost, and her eyes were frozen open. She could hardly move her fingers. But such was her determination to see an end, that she held out her palm, spoke a word of impulse, then pushed.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Her skin touched the stone. H}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 er mind ran flush with visions of her birth and her mother\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s rage, then the loss of her first love, and then the faces of all those who never saw in her what she saw in them. At the bottom was a plug of shame, and once loosed,  out came the thousand ages of her despair. The Black hungered for it. It swallowed it whole.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 It left her in a yawning stupor, barely able to sip on her bubble of air. She watched, emotionless, as the Black smashed into the atmosphere below. It fell into the clouds, then out of them. She did not aim it, but it knew of her great desire, for it fell upon the source of all conflict, all turmoil: the God of all creation, Him, the fallen star.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 The}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1  impact shook the earth.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 A shockwave swept the sky clean, even as it dragged a shade of dust behind it. When this wave found the sea, it steamed the waters, igniting a magnificent phytoplankton bloom, thousands of miles long, whirling in a gyre.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The soundlessness of space muted her sigh of awe.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Cold seized her lungs as the fireball mushroomed near her.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 She sensed the stretching of time, the thinness of her life. }{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 She wondered how long her body would last amongst the stars. She wondered how long she would cast her shadow upon the world. Last, she imagined a child hearing the story of the Impartial Third, and oh, how she wished that child would stare up into the night to look for her; whether it be in fear, or in anger, or to utter a warding curse, it did not matter, so long as they looked for her.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 But this was not her fate.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 She fell back down to the earth to find that mountain crag.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 She stepped up it\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s edge, then jumped. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Over the next several days, the rain of dust from the meteor\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s impact buried her body.}}